1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:09,560
Speaker 1: China's economy is, quote, slowing sharply as the trade ward enters its fifth month, while every other major trade partner has made a deal with Trump.

2
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:19,240
Speaker 1: Across the board, China's retail sales are slowing, industrial production missed expectations, investment is crawling at just half a percent, the worst since the 2020 pandemic.

3
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:24,020
Speaker 1: Urban joblessness, which is the only kind China cares about, is about a fifth higher than the U.S.

4
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:30,180
Speaker 1: Youth unemployment is nearly 18 percent, up three points in a single month.

5
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:33,100
Speaker 1: Still, the numbers are not as bad as many predicted.

6
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:36,080
Speaker 1: Unlike Mexico or Germany, China is not crashing.

7
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:37,580
Speaker 1: Now, this could be fake.

8
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:43,760
Speaker 1: China is famous for massaging statistics on a scale that puts our lying Bureau of Labor Statistics to shame.

9
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:53,400
Speaker 1: As the New York Times puts it, quote, China's ability to tightly control information has prevented unfettered conversations about the toll inflicted by the trade war.

10
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:58,540
Speaker 1: Did China stop publishing hundreds of economic statistics once the trade war began?

11
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:04,000
Speaker 1: Still, China's out in free fall for two reasons, stimulus and dumping.

12
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:19,000
Speaker 1: So far, China's dumped 1.6 trillion of stimulus to dull the pain, along with unconventional moves, like forcing 867,000 state-owned firms to buy their own shares, and, quote, investigating private brokerages who also sell shares.

13
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:24,140
Speaker 1: Second, China's actually increased exports to everybody but the U.S.

14
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:32,220
Speaker 1: Now, big hunk is tariff evasion, where Chinese ships stop in Vietnam, unload and reload, keep going to L.A., and presto, it's a Vietnam import.

15
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:38,580
Speaker 1: But another hunk is dumping the stuff they can't sell in America, below cost, into Europe and the third world.

16
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:43,320
Speaker 1: So, Temu's Latin American shipments have soared 143% this year.

17
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:49,100
Speaker 1: There was a recent uproar in Indonesia about Chinese jeans selling for 80 cents.

18
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:50,720
Speaker 1: None of this is sustainable.

19
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:57,540
Speaker 1: Trump's new deals are explicitly targeting reshipments, while countries are already pushing back on Chinese dumping.

20
00:01:58,020 --> 00:02:01,100
Speaker 1: The EU imposed new tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles.

21
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:02,820
Speaker 1: India is targeting Chinese steel.

22
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:05,100
Speaker 1: Mexico is targeting Chinese cars.

23
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:13,880
Speaker 1: In fact, China knows it's not sustainable, recently moving to starve marginal factories of capital to reduce overcapacity, in other words, to shut them down.

24
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:22,360
Speaker 1: In short, China has so far avoided the worst, assuming you believe, official numbers, but it's running out of runway on both stimulus and dumping.

25
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:32,300
Speaker 1: Now, China has 40 days, until November 11th, to make a deal with Trump or tariffs jump back to 145% up from 30% today.

26
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:37,480
Speaker 1: The outstanding issues include barriers to American food, energy, and services, forced tech transfers,

27
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:42,480
Speaker 1: where China forces foreign companies to share tech and even train their competitors,

28
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:47,540
Speaker 1: along with so-called industrial policy, where Beijing gives cheap money to exporters.

29
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:52,540
Speaker 1: And Trump being Trump, he will probably want guaranteed Chinese purchases of American goods.

30
00:02:53,220 --> 00:02:57,140
Speaker 1: Last time around, it was $200 billion, which they never bought using COVID as the excuse.

31
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:59,520
Speaker 1: So as sags brought to buy, I trust capital.

32
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:00,960
Speaker 1: Both sides seem motivated.

33
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:05,960
Speaker 1: China backed off a ban on rare earth exports, which are used to make American weapons.

34
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:10,540
Speaker 1: A single F-35 contains a half ton of rare earths.

35
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:13,860
Speaker 1: Meanwhile, China's rhetoric has been uncharacteristically tame.

36
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:17,540
Speaker 1: They're no longer calling Trump a self-serving narcissistic bandit.

37
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:26,600
Speaker 1: For his part, Trump just postponed a planned arms sale to Taiwan and could even shift America's Taiwan stance from neutrality to opposing independence,

38
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:28,660
Speaker 1: which is a holy grail for China.

39
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,320
Speaker 1: So most likely, they will come to an agreement by November.

40
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:38,760
Speaker 1: And if they don't, China goes from a 30% tariff slowdown to a 145% collapse.

41
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:40,480
Speaker 1: Okay, we'll be watching.

42
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:41,460
Speaker 1: See you next time.
